-- Q3 [Tertiary Structure & Folding] medium skill2 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Tertiary Structure & Folding', 'As a globular protein folds, a nonpolar leucine side chain moves from the aqueous surface into the protein''s tightly packed interior. This burial is a major driving force for folding. Which change in the surrounding solvent makes this step thermodynamically favorable?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Ordered water cages form, lowering solvent entropy"}, {"label": "B", "text": "New backbone hydrogen bonds lower the system enthalpy"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Ordered cage water is released, raising solvent entropy"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Cysteine thiols oxidize, forming a covalent crosslink"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is C because burying a nonpolar side chain releases the ordered, ''ice-like'' water cage that had surrounded it when it was solvent-exposed, and the textbook states that this dissipation of the cavity increases the number of microstates available to the released bulk water, an entropy gain that makes the process spontaneous: "transferring the hydrocarbon from water to the pure liquid dissipates the cavity, thereby increasing the number of available microstates for the released solvent, bulk water" (libretexts-biochemistry.txt, lines 4749-4752). The hydrophobic effect is represented in the folding free-energy diagram as the favorable entropy term \(-T\Delta S \ll 0\) and is named a largest contributor to native-state stabilization (libretexts-biochemistry.txt, lines 14714-14716, 14692). (Choice A) reverses the sign of the entropy change: forming ordered water cages around an exposed nonpolar group lowers solvent entropy and is the unfavorable, disfavored direction, not the one that drives burial (libretexts-biochemistry.txt, lines 4746-4748). (Choice B) is a partial truth that names the wrong driving force and the wrong structural level — backbone hydrogen bonds belong to secondary structure and contribute through enthalpy \(\Delta H \ll 0\), but the question asks about the solvent change that makes hydrophobic burial favorable, which is the entropic term \(-T\Delta S \ll 0\) (libretexts-biochemistry.txt, lines 14718-14719, 11243-11244). (Choice C) is correct: releasing the structured cage water raises solvent entropy and drives folding (libretexts-biochemistry.txt, lines 4749-4752). (Choice D) describes disulfide bond formation, a separate covalent tertiary-structure interaction formed by oxidizing two cysteine thiols, which does not involve the solvent-entropy change that makes nonpolar burial favorable (libretexts-biochemistry.txt, lines 5892-5894). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the thermodynamics of the hydrophobic effect to predict the sign of the solvent entropy change for burying a nonpolar residue.', 'medium', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
