-- Q4 [Tertiary Structure & Folding] medium skill2 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Tertiary Structure & Folding', 'A researcher treats a purified, correctly folded extracellular enzyme with dithiothreitol (DTT), a reducing agent, and observes that the protein partially unfolds even though no peptide bonds are hydrolyzed. Which tertiary-stabilizing interaction is most directly disrupted by this treatment?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "A backbone hydrogen bond within an alpha-helix"}, {"label": "B", "text": "A salt bridge between charged side chains"}, {"label": "C", "text": "A hydrophobic contact in the protein core"}, {"label": "D", "text": "A covalent disulfide bond between two cysteines"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is D because a disulfide bond is a covalent \(-\text{S}-\text{S}-\) linkage formed when two cysteine thiols are oxidized to give cystine, and reducing agents such as dithiothreitol specifically cleave these bonds. The textbook states that "two cysteine side chains can covalently interact in a protein to form a disulfide bond (RS-SR), called cystine," where "RSSR is the oxidized form ... and RSH is the reduced form" (LibreTexts 5892–5894), and that disulfides "can be cleaved by reducing agents such as beta-mercaptoethanol, dithiothreitol, or tris(2-carboxyethyl)phosphine (TCEP)" (LibreTexts 5953–5955). Because disulfide bonds "stabilize both tertiary and quaternary structures" (LibreTexts 6613), reducing them removes a covalent cross-link and lets the protein partially unfold without breaking the primary sequence. (Choice A) is wrong because a backbone hydrogen bond stabilizes secondary structure (the alpha-helix), not tertiary structure, and DTT reduces disulfides rather than disrupting hydrogen bonds. (Choice B) is wrong because a salt bridge is a noncovalent ion-ion interaction between charged side chains (LibreTexts 3938–3940) and is not the target of a thiol-reducing agent. (Choice C) is wrong because a hydrophobic contact is a noncovalent interaction driven by burying nonpolar residues away from water, and DTT does not reduce or cleave it. (Choice D) is correct: DTT reduces the oxidized cystine cross-link back to two free cysteine thiols, eliminating the only covalent bond that forms during folding and destabilizing the tertiary structure. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the redox chemistry of cysteine and the action of a reducing agent to predict which specific stabilizing interaction is lost.', 'medium', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
