-- Q5 [Tertiary Structure & Folding] hard skill2 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Tertiary Structure & Folding', 'A point mutation in a globular enzyme distorts its core so that a leucine side chain that is normally buried becomes exposed to the surrounding water. Considering only this newly water-exposed nonpolar side chain, how does forcing it into contact with water affect the entropy of the solvent and the free-energy change of that exposure step (at constant temperature)?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Solvent \\(\\Delta S<0\\); exposure \\(\\Delta G>0\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Solvent \\(\\Delta S>0\\); exposure \\(\\Delta G<0\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Solvent \\(\\Delta S>0\\); exposure \\(\\Delta G>0\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Solvent \\(\\Delta S<0\\); exposure \\(\\Delta G<0\\)"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is A because exposing a buried nonpolar side chain to water forces the surrounding water into a more ordered "cavity" or cage to preserve its hydrogen bonding, a state with fewer microstates and lower positional entropy, so the solvent entropy change is negative \(\Delta S<0\). The textbook states that "a literal cavity must be created to accommodate a hydrocarbon in water. The creation of this more ordered cavity must be entropically disfavored (...the process proceeds to a state with fewer microstates and lower positional entropy)" (libretexts-biochemistry.txt, lines 4746-4748) and that water "is forced into a more ordered structure around the HC... characterized by fewer microstates" (lines 4753-4755). This entropy loss is the dominant unfavorable contribution, so even though placing a nonpolar group in water is enthalpically favored, the transfer is overall disfavored, \(\Delta G>0\) (lines 4739-4743). By \(\Delta G=\Delta H-T\Delta S\) (line 4827) the negative \(\Delta S\) makes the \(-T\Delta S\) term positive and the exposure step nonspontaneous; the reverse process (burying the residue) releases ordered water for a "+\(\Delta S\)" and is the favorable, folding-driving direction (lines 4749-4752, 4821-4822, 4783-4786). (Choice A) is correct: ordering water around the exposed nonpolar group lowers solvent entropy (\(\Delta S<0\)), making exposure unfavorable (\(\Delta G>0\)). (Choice B) reverses both signs; \(\Delta S>0\) with \(\Delta G<0\) describes burying the residue and releasing ordered water, the opposite (favorable) direction the text assigns "+\(\Delta S\)" (lines 4749-4752, 4821-4822). (Choice C) keeps the correct unfavorable \(\Delta G>0\) but wrongly claims solvent \(\Delta S>0\); exposing the nonpolar group orders the water rather than disordering it, so \(\Delta S<0\) (lines 4746-4748). (Choice D) correctly takes \(\Delta S<0\) but then claims \(\Delta G<0\); a negative solvent entropy change makes \(-T\Delta S\) positive, so by \(\Delta G=\Delta H-T\Delta S\) the entropy-dominated exposure step is unfavorable, \(\Delta G>0\), not spontaneous (lines 4747-4748, 4827). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the hydrophobic-effect entropy argument and the Gibbs relation to predict the sign of \(\Delta S\) and \(\Delta G\) for a specific exposed residue.', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 160)
  RETURNING id
)
