-- Q6 [Tertiary Structure & Folding] medium skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Tertiary Structure & Folding', 'A globular enzyme is stabilized in part by a salt bridge that helps clamp two distant segments of its folded chain together. A biochemist mapping these contacts wants to identify a residue pair whose side chains form this electrostatic interaction at physiological pH. Which pairing of R groups forms the salt bridge?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Two cysteine side chains"}, {"label": "B", "text": "A glutamate and a lysine side chain"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Two aspartate side chains"}, {"label": "D", "text": "A lysine and a phenylalanine side chain"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is B because a salt bridge is an ion-ion (electrostatic) interaction that forms when an acidic, negatively charged side chain comes close in 3D space to a basic, positively charged side chain within the folded protein. The textbook states that "Ion-Ion (salt bridge) interactions between acidic amino acid side chains (Glu- and Asp-) and alkaline amino acid side chains (Arg+, Lys+, and His+) are the strongest residue-residue interactions" and illustrates one "between the side chains of two amino acids, aspartic acid (Asp)... and lysine (Lys)." Glutamate carries a negatively charged carboxylate and lysine a positively charged ammonium at physiological pH, so the two opposite charges attract to bridge distant parts of the chain. (Choice A) is incorrect: two cysteine side chains form a covalent disulfide bond (cystine, \(-\text{S}-\text{S}-\)) by oxidation of their thiols, which is a covalent linkage, not an electrostatic salt bridge. (Choice B) is correct: glutamate (acidic, negatively charged) paired with lysine (basic, positively charged) gives the opposite-charge attraction that defines a salt bridge. (Choice C) is incorrect: two aspartate side chains are both acidic and negatively charged, so they repel rather than attract; a salt bridge requires one positive and one negative partner. (Choice D) is incorrect: a positively charged lysine paired with the aromatic ring of phenylalanine is a cation-π interaction, not an ion-ion salt bridge, because phenylalanine bears no formal charge. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definition of a salt bridge to specific side-chain pairs and determine which combination provides the required opposite charges.', 'medium', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
