-- Q7 [Tertiary Structure & Folding] hard skill2 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Tertiary Structure & Folding', 'A globular enzyme whose native fold is stabilized by two intrachain disulfide bonds is incubated with excess dithiothreitol (DTT), a reducing agent, under non-denaturing buffer conditions. Which outcome at the molecular level best accounts for the loss of catalytic activity that follows?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Peptide bonds in the backbone are hydrolyzed"}, {"label": "B", "text": "New disulfide bridges form, over-rigidifying the fold"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Cystine crosslinks are reduced, destabilizing tertiary structure"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Backbone hydrogen bonds of the helices are broken"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is C because a disulfide bond (cystine) is a covalent \(-\text{S}-\text{S}-\) crosslink formed by oxidation of two cysteine thiols, and it is one of the R-group interactions that stabilize a protein''s overall three-dimensional tertiary structure. A reducing agent such as DTT (or beta-mercaptoethanol) cleaves these disulfides, regenerating free thiols (cystine to cysteine); the textbook notes that ''disulfides can be cleaved by reducing agents such as beta-mercaptoethanol, dithiothreitol, or tris(2-carboxyethyl)phosphine (TCEP)'' and that ''disulfide bonds within individual chains and between them stabilize both tertiary and quaternary structures.'' Removing a stabilizing covalent crosslink unfolds the native tertiary structure, and loss of three-dimensional shape means the protein ''may no longer be functional.'' (Choice A) is incorrect: reducing agents act on the \(-\text{S}-\text{S}-\) bond, not on the backbone amide bonds; denaturation occurs ''without losing its primary sequence,'' so peptide bonds remain intact. (Choice B) is incorrect because it reverses the redox direction—DTT is a reducing agent that breaks disulfides, whereas disulfide formation requires oxidation ''in the presence of oxygen''; no new bridges form. (Choice C) is correct: the reducing agent reduces the cystine crosslinks, removing a covalent stabilizer of the tertiary fold and causing it to unfold. (Choice D) is incorrect because the backbone hydrogen bonds that define secondary structure (alpha-helices, beta-sheets) are not the target of a thiol-reducing agent; DTT acts specifically on the cysteine-derived disulfide, an R-group covalent bond, not on backbone H-bonds. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the chemistry of a reducing agent to predict its effect on the bond it targets and on the level of protein structure that bond stabilizes.', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
