-- Q9 [Tertiary Structure & Folding] hard skill1 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Tertiary Structure & Folding', 'A globular enzyme folds in the oxidizing environment of the endoplasmic reticulum, where free cysteine thiols are oxidized to crosslinks. A researcher cataloging the forces that lock in this protein''s overall three-dimensional shape considers the following interactions:

I. Nonpolar R groups cluster together in the protein''s water-excluded core.
II. Hydrogen bonds between backbone amide hydrogens and carbonyl oxygens repeat at regular intervals along one segment.
III. Two cysteine side chains are oxidized to form a covalent \(-\text{S}-\text{S}-\) crosslink.

Which of these interactions stabilize the protein''s TERTIARY structure?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "I and III only"}, {"label": "B", "text": "I only"}, {"label": "C", "text": "III only"}, {"label": "D", "text": "II and III only"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is A because tertiary structure is the overall 3D shape of a single polypeptide, driven by side-chain (R-group) interactions: the hydrophobic effect buries nonpolar R groups in a water-excluded core (statement I), and the oxidation of two cysteine thiols to a covalent disulfide (statement III) further stabilizes that fold. LibreTexts states that on folding ''the bulk of nonpolar groups [are] buried and surrounded by other nonpolar groups'' while ''polar and charged groups [are] on the surface,'' and that ''Two cysteine side chains can covalently interact in a protein to form a disulfide bond (RS-SR), called cystine,'' where ''RSSR is the oxidized form ... of thiols.'' Statement II describes backbone hydrogen bonding ''at regular intervals,'' which LibreTexts defines as SECONDARY structure, not tertiary: ''The secondary structure is the repetitive structure formed from H-bonds among backbone amide H and carbonyl O atoms.'' (Choice A) is correct: only the R-group hydrophobic packing (I) and the disulfide crosslink (III) are tertiary-stabilizing R-group interactions. (Choice B) is wrong because it drops the covalent disulfide bond (III), which LibreTexts says ''stabilize[s] both tertiary and quaternary structures.'' (Choice C) is wrong because it omits hydrophobic core packing (I), the main thermodynamic driving force for folding. (Choice D) is wrong because it includes statement II (backbone H-bonding), which is the defining feature of secondary structure, and excludes the hydrophobic effect (I). This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall which specific interactions define and stabilize tertiary versus secondary structure.', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
