-- Q11 [Quaternary Structure & Cooperativity] easy skill1 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Quaternary Structure & Cooperativity', 'A researcher purifies a functional enzyme and finds that the active protein is built from four separate polypeptide chains held together by noncovalent interactions. This arrangement of multiple chains into one functional protein represents which level of protein structure?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Tertiary structure"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Quaternary structure"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Secondary structure"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Primary structure"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is B because quaternary structure is, by definition, the level of organization that arises when two or more separate polypeptide chains (subunits) assemble into a single functional protein. The textbook states that "Quaternary structure is the overall structure that arises when separate protein chains aggregate with themselves to form homodimers, homotrimers, or homopolymers, or aggregate with different proteins to form heteropolymers" and that these subunits are "held together by noncovalent interactions" (LibreTexts 4.3.4, lines 11244-11247). A four-chain (tetrameric) functional assembly held by noncovalent interactions is therefore a quaternary structure. (Choice A) Tertiary structure is "the overall 3D structure of the protein" of a single chain (LibreTexts 4.3.1, line 11058); it describes the folding of one chain, not the joining of multiple separate chains, so it is wrong. (Choice B) is correct: assembly of multiple separate subunits into one functional protein is quaternary structure (LibreTexts lines 11244-11247; OpenStax line 4456: "some proteins form from several polypeptides, or subunits, and the interaction of these subunits forms the quaternary structure"). (Choice C) Secondary structure is "the repetitive structure formed from H-bonds among backbone amide H and carbonyl O atoms" within a chain (LibreTexts line 11243), not the joining of separate chains, so it is wrong. (Choice D) Primary structure is "the linear sequence of the protein" (LibreTexts line 11243) — the order of amino acids in one chain — not a multi-subunit assembly, so it is wrong. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the definition of quaternary structure and distinguish it from the other levels of protein organization.', 'easy', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 70)
  RETURNING id
)
