-- Q12 [Quaternary Structure & Cooperativity] medium skill2 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Quaternary Structure & Cooperativity', 'A researcher purifies a newly characterized enzyme and determines that the functional, active form of the protein consists of a single folded polypeptide chain with no other associated chains. Of the four levels of protein organization, which level does this enzyme NOT possess?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Primary structure"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Tertiary structure"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Quaternary structure"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Secondary structure"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is C because quaternary structure is defined as the assembly of two or more polypeptide subunits, so a protein whose functional form is a single chain cannot have it. The textbook states that quaternary structure "is the overall structure that arises when separate protein chains aggregate with themselves... or aggregate with different proteins," and is "the assembly of two or more protein subunits"; it is precisely this multi-subunit assembly that enables cooperativity (e.g., hemoglobin''s four subunits), whereas a single-chain protein such as myoglobin lacks it. Because the enzyme described is one folded chain, it has primary, secondary, and tertiary structure but stops short of quaternary. (Choice A) is incorrect because primary structure is simply the linear amino acid sequence, which every polypeptide possesses regardless of subunit number. (Choice B) is incorrect because tertiary structure is "the overall three-dimensional folding of a single polypeptide chain," exactly what one folded chain has. (Choice C) is correct: with only one polypeptide there are no separate subunits to assemble, so no quaternary structure exists. (Choice D) is incorrect because secondary structure (the local backbone hydrogen-bonded \(\alpha\)-helices and \(\beta\)-sheets) forms within a single chain and is present here. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definition of each structural level to a described protein to deduce which one a single-chain protein cannot have.', 'medium', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
