-- Q13 [Quaternary Structure & Cooperativity] hard skill2 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Quaternary Structure & Cooperativity', 'Normal hemoglobin is a heterotetramer of two \(\alpha\) and two \(\beta\) subunits; the binding of \(\text{O}_2\) to one subunit triggers a conformational change that is transmitted across the subunit interfaces, raising the \(\text{O}_2\) affinity of the remaining subunits. A point mutation at an \(\alpha\)–\(\beta\) interface yields a variant whose four subunits still fold and bind \(\text{O}_2\) individually but no longer transmit this change between subunits. Compared with normal hemoglobin, the mutant is expected to show:', '[{"label": "A", "text": "a denatured, fully unfolded tetramer"}, {"label": "B", "text": "loss of its heme prosthetic groups"}, {"label": "C", "text": "a stronger, more cooperative response"}, {"label": "D", "text": "reduced cooperativity between the subunits"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is D because cooperativity (allostery) depends on a conformational change in one subunit being transmitted across the subunit interfaces to alter the other subunits'' affinity; when an allosteric activator binds a multi-subunit protein, the induced conformational change increases the affinity of the other active sites for substrate (OpenStax lines 8580–8585: "When an allosteric inhibitor binds to an enzyme, all active sites on the protein subunits change slightly... Allosteric activators bind to locations on an enzyme away from the active site, inducing a conformational change that increases the affinity of the enzyme''s active site(s) for its substrate(s)"). Hemoglobin is a heterotetramer of two alpha and two beta subunits whose interfaces enable this signaling (LibreTexts lines 11273–11274: "Hemoglobin consists of two alpha and two beta monomers... which assemble to produce the biologically relevant heterotetrameric protein"). An interface mutation that lets each subunit still fold and bind \(\text{O}_2\) but blocks transmission of the conformational change therefore removes the affinity-raising signal between subunits, so the subunits behave more independently and cooperativity falls. (Choice A) is incorrect: denaturation is the loss of 3D structure and function, but the stem states the subunits still fold and bind \(\text{O}_2\), so the protein is not unfolded (LibreTexts lines 11243–11244: "The tertiary structure is the protein''s overall 3D structure"). (Choice B) is incorrect: the heme is a prosthetic group held within each subunit, and a single interface substitution that preserves individual binding does not strip the heme; the stem confirms each subunit still binds \(\text{O}_2\) (LibreTexts lines 20962–20963: hemoglobin''s subunits are "each containing a heme group. The heme prosthetic group...is essential for binding dioxygen"). (Choice C) is incorrect and reverses the relationship: a stronger cooperative response would require more signal transmission, but the mutation abolishes transmission between subunits, so cooperativity decreases rather than increases (OpenStax lines 8582–8585). (Choice D) is correct: with the inter-subunit conformational signal broken, binding at one subunit no longer raises the affinity of the others, which is precisely a loss of cooperativity (OpenStax lines 8580–8585). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the mechanism of inter-subunit conformational transmission to predict how an interface mutation changes the multi-subunit binding behavior.', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
