-- Q14 [Quaternary Structure & Cooperativity] medium skill2 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Quaternary Structure & Cooperativity', 'A researcher studying a multi-subunit metabolic enzyme notices that an intermediate generated at the active site of one subunit appears to pass directly to the active site of a neighboring subunit without diffusing into the bulk solvent. This observation would most directly illustrate which functional advantage of quaternary structure?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Positioning separate subunits'' active sites for intermediate channeling"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Encoding the linear sequence of amino acid residues"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Forming backbone hydrogen bonds that define secondary structure"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Folding a single chain into its 3D shape"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is A because quaternary structure arises when separate protein chains aggregate into oligomers, and one functional implication of this assembly is substrate channeling. The textbook lists the ''functional implications of symmetric arrangements in oligomers (e.g., kinetic regulation, substrate channeling, and structural stability)'' (line 11741) and explains channeling as a reaction product ''diffus[ing] to another catalytic domain (or interface between domains),'' which ''reduce[s] the dimensionality of the search for a substrate from 3D to more of a 1D or 2D search, enormously speeding up the net reaction'' (lines 12085-12087). Bringing the active sites of adjacent subunits into proximity so an intermediate passes between them without escaping into solvent is precisely this quaternary-level benefit. (Choice A) is correct: handing off an intermediate between the active sites of neighboring subunits is the channeling advantage conferred by assembling subunits into a quaternary complex. (Choice B) is wrong because encoding the linear sequence of amino acid residues describes primary structure, not quaternary structure; the textbook states ''The primary structure is the linear sequence of the protein'' (line 11243). (Choice C) is wrong because backbone hydrogen bonds that define secondary structure are a feature of secondary structure: ''The secondary structure is the repetitive structure formed from H-bonds among backbone amide H and carbonyl O atoms'' (lines 11243-11244). (Choice D) is wrong because folding a single chain into its 3D shape describes tertiary structure: ''The tertiary structure is the protein''s overall 3D structure'' (line 11244), and a single chain has no quaternary structure. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definition and functional consequences of quaternary structure to interpret an experimental observation about intermediate transfer between subunit active sites.', 'medium', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
