-- Q15 [Quaternary Structure & Cooperativity] hard skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Quaternary Structure & Cooperativity', 'A researcher is comparing single-subunit proteins with multi-subunit (oligomeric) proteins to explain why so many functional proteins are assembled from several identical polypeptide subunits. She lists features that are supposed to be genuine consequences of forming a quaternary structure. Which of the following statements correctly describes such a consequence?

I. The subunits of the assembled protein are covalently joined to one another by peptide bonds linking their backbones.
II. Cooperative ligand binding requires that each subunit bind its ligand fully independently of the other subunits.
III. A single advantageous mutation in the gene is reproduced n times across a symmetric homo-oligomer of n identical subunits.', '[{"label": "A", "text": "I only"}, {"label": "B", "text": "III only"}, {"label": "C", "text": "I and III only"}, {"label": "D", "text": "II and III only"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is B because only statement III names a real benefit of quaternary assembly. Quaternary structure arises when separate polypeptide chains aggregate, and these subunits are held together mainly by noncovalent interactions, not by peptide bonds. Because a symmetric homo-oligomer is built from identical subunits, a single mutation in one monomer is represented n times in an oligomer of n monomers, so one favorable change in a single gene product is reused throughout the assembly. Cooperativity, by contrast, is defined by subunits NOT binding independently: binding of the first ligand facilitates binding of the next. (Choice A) is wrong because statement I is false — subunits in a quaternary structure are held together predominantly by noncovalent interactions, not by backbone peptide bonds (peptide bonds define primary structure within a single chain). (Choice B) is correct: statement III captures the genetic economy of symmetric homo-oligomers, where one beneficial mutation in a single gene is propagated n times across the n identical subunits, while statements I and II are false. (Choice C) is wrong because it includes the false statement I; although III is correct, subunits are not joined by peptide bonds. (Choice D) is wrong because it includes the false statement II; cooperativity is precisely the opposite of independent binding — occupancy of one site alters the affinity of the remaining sites. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definitions of the structural hierarchy and cooperativity to evaluate which proposed consequences of quaternary assembly are actually valid.', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
