-- Q16 [Quaternary Structure & Cooperativity] easy skill1 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Quaternary Structure & Cooperativity', 'In a four-subunit protein, binding of \(\text{O}_2\) to one subunit triggers a conformational change that raises the \(\text{O}_2\) affinity of the remaining subunits. This regulation, in which a shape change in one subunit alters the activity of the others, is termed:', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Denaturation of the complex"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The hydrophobic effect"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Allosteric regulation"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Peptide bond formation"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is C because allosterism (allosteric regulation) occurs when a ligand-binding event produces a conformational change at one site that alters the binding affinity or activity at the protein''s other subunits. The textbook gives this exact example: "oxygen binding to one of the subunits of hemoglobin that prompts cooperative binding to other subunits" is described as allostery, and notes that "as oxygen binds, the next oxygen seems to bind with higher affinity (lower KD)." Because the conformational change in one subunit must be communicated to the others, this cooperative form of allosteric regulation requires more than one polypeptide subunit (quaternary structure). (Choice A) Denaturation is the loss of higher-order (tertiary/quaternary) structure and function, not a regulatory communication between intact subunits. (Choice B) The hydrophobic effect buries nonpolar side chains in the core during folding and stabilizes tertiary structure; it does not describe one subunit regulating another. (Choice C) is correct: a conformational change in one subunit that changes the affinity/activity of the other subunits is the definition of allosteric (cooperative) regulation. (Choice D) Peptide bond formation joins amino acids into the primary sequence and has nothing to do with subunit-to-subunit affinity changes. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the definition of allosteric regulation and how it arises from quaternary structure.', 'easy', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 70)
  RETURNING id
)
