-- Q17 [Quaternary Structure & Cooperativity] hard skill2 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Quaternary Structure & Cooperativity', 'A researcher treats hemoglobin, a tetramer of four \(\text{O}_2\)-binding subunits whose saturation curve is sigmoidal, with a mild detergent that dissociates the assembly into isolated, fully folded monomers, each still bearing an intact heme. Each isolated monomer is then assayed for \(\text{O}_2\) binding. Compared with the intact tetramer, the isolated monomers will most likely show:', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Sigmoidal binding with higher cooperativity"}, {"label": "B", "text": "No detectable binding of \\(\\text{O}_2\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Sigmoidal binding but lower affinity"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Hyperbolic, non-cooperative binding"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is D because cooperativity is a property of the assembled quaternary structure: when subunits are packed together in the tetramer, binding of \(\text{O}_2\) at one heme drives compensatory conformational changes that the tightly packed neighbors must accommodate, so the first binding event facilitates the next (a sigmoidal curve). The textbook notes that plots of fractional saturation are ''hyperbolic for myoglobin (Mb), but sigmoidal for Hb,'' and that cooperativity arises because ''the subunits are packed so tightly that compensatory changes in their arrangement must occur.'' Once the tetramer is dissociated into isolated monomers, there are no neighboring subunits to communicate with, so each monomer behaves like single-chain myoglobin: it still binds \(\text{O}_2\) through its intact heme, but with a simple hyperbolic, non-cooperative curve. (Choice A) is incorrect because cooperativity cannot increase after the inter-subunit contacts that produce it are eliminated; an isolated monomer has nothing to cooperate with. (Choice B) is incorrect because each monomer retains its folded tertiary structure and intact heme, so it still binds \(\text{O}_2\) — only the cooperative behavior is lost, not binding itself. (Choice C) is incorrect because binding cannot remain sigmoidal once the multi-subunit assembly is gone; sigmoidicity requires the quaternary structure, which dissociation removes. (Choice D) is correct: dissociating the cooperative multimer abolishes inter-subunit communication, leaving each monomer to bind \(\text{O}_2\) hyperbolically and independently, exactly as myoglobin does. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that cooperativity depends on quaternary assembly to predict the binding behavior of the dissociated monomers.', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
