-- Q18 [Quaternary Structure & Cooperativity] medium skill2 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Quaternary Structure & Cooperativity', 'A researcher studies a multi-subunit enzyme whose four polypeptide chains assemble into a functional tetramer. The chains carry no free thiols available for cross-linking between them. Which interactions are predominantly responsible for holding these separate subunits together in the assembled complex?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Noncovalent interactions between subunits"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Peptide bonds linking the chains"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Backbone amide–carbonyl hydrogen bonds"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Interchain disulfide bonds between subunits"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is A because quaternary structure—the arrangement of two or more separate polypeptide subunits into the functional protein—is held together predominantly by noncovalent interactions (intermolecular forces). The textbook states that "most protein subunits in a larger protein displaying quaternary structure are held together by noncovalent interactions (intermolecular forces)" and gives hemoglobin as "a heterotetramer of two α and two β subunits held together by noncovalent interactions (an example of quaternary protein structure)." Interchain disulfide bonds can also contribute "in some cases," but those require free cysteine thiols on different chains, which this enzyme lacks—leaving noncovalent forces as the dominant interaction. (Choice A) is correct: separate chains aggregate mainly through noncovalent interactions, which is exactly how the assembled subunits of proteins like hemoglobin are held together. (Choice B) is wrong because peptide bonds covalently link amino acids in sequence within a single chain to form primary structure; they do not join one finished subunit to another. (Choice C) is wrong because backbone amide-hydrogen and carbonyl-oxygen hydrogen bonds define secondary structure (α-helices and β-sheets) within a chain, not the inter-subunit contacts of quaternary structure. (Choice D) is wrong because interchain disulfide bonds form only between free cysteine side chains on different chains and stabilize quaternary structure in some cases (e.g., immunoglobulins); the stem specifies no free thiols are available, so this cannot be the predominant force here. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the rule that quaternary structure is held mainly by noncovalent interactions to a specific protein that lacks the free thiols needed for the disulfide alternative.', 'medium', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
