-- Q19 [Conjugated Proteins & Denaturation] easy skill1 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Conjugated Proteins & Denaturation', 'Hemoglobin transports \(\text{O}_2\) only because each subunit carries a heme group whose iron binds the gas; this classifies hemoglobin as a conjugated protein. A protein is termed conjugated when it contains which of the following features?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "An internal disulfide bond between two cysteines"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Two or more identical polypeptide subunits"}, {"label": "C", "text": "A bound non-peptide prosthetic group"}, {"label": "D", "text": "A repeating backbone-stabilized secondary motif"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is C because a conjugated protein is one that, beyond its polypeptide chain(s), carries a bound non-peptide component called a prosthetic group; OpenStax states that the term protein applies to polypeptides that "often have bound non-peptide prosthetic groups," and it gives hemoglobin''s and cytochrome c''s heme as the classic example, noting "this protein has a heme prosthetic group." LibreTexts similarly lists "prosthetic groups" among the fundamental building blocks distinct from amino acids and describes hemoglobin''s "heme prosthetic group—consisting of protoporphyrin IX and a centrally coordinated Fe²⁺ ion." The heme is the non-peptide moiety that makes hemoglobin a conjugated protein. (Choice A) An internal disulfide bond links two cysteine R-groups and stabilizes tertiary structure, but it is still part of the polypeptide itself, not an attached non-peptide group, so it does not make a protein conjugated. (Choice B) Two or more polypeptide subunits define quaternary structure, not conjugation; a multi-subunit protein made only of amino acids is still a simple (non-conjugated) protein. (Choice C) is correct: a bound non-peptide prosthetic group, such as heme, is exactly what distinguishes a conjugated protein from a simple one. (Choice D) A repeating, backbone-hydrogen-bonded motif describes secondary structure (an α-helix or β-sheet), which is built from the peptide backbone and is unrelated to whether a non-peptide group is attached. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the definition of a conjugated protein and its prosthetic group.', 'easy', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 70)
  RETURNING id
)
