WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Conjugated Proteins & Denaturation', 'In each subunit of hemoglobin, a heme prosthetic group positions a central iron ion that picks up molecular oxygen in the lungs and releases it in respiring tissue. Which property of this iron center allows the same subunit to load and unload \(\text{O}_2\) repeatedly?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "It is covalently attached to a cysteine thiol"}, {"label": "B", "text": "It cycles between \\(\\text{Fe}^{2+}\\) and \\(\\text{Fe}^{3+}\\) states"}, {"label": "C", "text": "It forms hydrogen bonds with dioxygen"}, {"label": "D", "text": "It binds \\(\\text{O}_2\\) reversibly as ferrous \\(\\text{Fe}^{2+}\\)"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is D because the heme of hemoglobin holds a central ferrous \(\text{Fe}^{2+}\) ion that forms a coordinate covalent bond with dioxygen, and this binding is reversible: \(\text{O}_2\) is picked up where its concentration is high and released where it is low. The textbook states that ''Dioxygen binds to a heme Fe2+ transition metal through a coordinate covalent or dative bond'' and that ''the oxidation state of the Fe2+ ion does not change... Hence, the Fe2+ ion is a reversible carrier of dioxygen and not electrons.'' Because the iron stays in the +2 state and the bond can dissociate, the same subunit can load and unload \(\text{O}_2\) many times. (Choice A) is incorrect: the heme iron is held by coordinate covalent bonds to four pyrrole nitrogens and the proximal histidine, not by a covalent bond to a cysteine thiol; the textbook notes the proximal His ''serves as the 5th ligand to the heme Fe2+'' and that the porphyrin ''has a nitrogen atom on each of the four pyrrole rings that form a coordinate covalent bond to Fe2+.'' (Choice B) is incorrect: cycling between \(\text{Fe}^{2+}\) and \(\text{Fe}^{3+}\) describes the heme iron of cytochrome c during electron transport, where ''the heme''s central ion alternately reduces and oxidizes during electron transfer''; in oxygen-carrying hemoglobin the \(\text{Fe}^{2+}\) oxidation state does not change. (Choice C) is incorrect: \(\text{O}_2\) is bound by a coordinate covalent (dative) bond in which the ligand donates both electrons to the iron, not by a hydrogen bond. (Choice D) is correct: the heme iron remains ferrous \(\text{Fe}^{2+}\) and serves as a reversible carrier of dioxygen, exactly the property that permits repeated loading and unloading. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the structure of the heme prosthetic group and the oxidation state and reversible binding behavior of its central iron.', 'medium', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
