-- Q22 [Conjugated Proteins & Denaturation] easy skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Conjugated Proteins & Denaturation', 'A cell-surface protein is isolated and found to carry a covalently attached carbohydrate chain as its prosthetic group. Based on the identity of this prosthetic group, this conjugated protein is most accurately classified as which of the following?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "A lipoprotein"}, {"label": "B", "text": "A glycoprotein"}, {"label": "C", "text": "A metalloprotein"}, {"label": "D", "text": "A nucleoprotein"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is B because conjugated proteins are named for the chemical identity of the non-peptide prosthetic group they carry, and a protein bearing a covalently attached carbohydrate is a glycoprotein. The textbook states that carbohydrates are "attached to proteins, forming glycoproteins, or attached to lipids, forming glycolipids" (OpenStax Biology 2e, line 6593), and notes that proteins "often have bound non-peptide prosthetic groups" (OpenStax Biology 2e, line 4305); LibreTexts adds that proteins are post-translationally modified "by the attachment of carbohydrates ... usually attached through either Asn or Ser side chains" (LibreTexts Biochemistry, lines 35011-35012). Because the prosthetic group here is a carbohydrate (not a lipid, metal, or nucleic acid), the correct classification is glycoprotein. (Choice A) A lipoprotein carries a lipid prosthetic group; the textbook contrasts carbohydrates attached to proteins (glycoproteins) with carbohydrates attached to lipids (glycolipids) (OpenStax Biology 2e, line 6593), and lipoproteins such as LDL/HDL are lipid-bearing particles (OpenStax Biology 2e, lines 4055-4056), so this names the wrong prosthetic group. (Choice B) is correct: a covalently attached carbohydrate prosthetic group defines a glycoprotein (OpenStax Biology 2e, line 6593; LibreTexts Biochemistry, lines 35011-35012). (Choice C) A metalloprotein carries a metal ion as its prosthetic group — for example a heme protein such as cytochrome c, whose "heme prosthetic group" holds a central metal ion (OpenStax Biology 2e, lines 4322-4323; LibreTexts Biochemistry, line 19821 describes delivering the correct metal to metalloproteins) — not a carbohydrate. (Choice D) A nucleoprotein carries a nucleic acid component (e.g., ribonucleoprotein complexes; LibreTexts Biochemistry, line 676), not a carbohydrate. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the rule that a conjugated protein is classified by the chemical identity of its prosthetic group to assign the correct category from a described scenario.', 'easy', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
