-- Q24 [Conjugated Proteins & Denaturation] medium skill2 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Conjugated Proteins & Denaturation', 'An investigator unfolds purified ribonuclease (a single-chain enzyme with four intrachain disulfide bonds) by treating it with 8 M urea plus the reducing agent \(\beta\)-mercaptoethanol, abolishing its catalytic activity. The \(\beta\)-mercaptoethanol is then removed by dialysis under oxidizing conditions, and finally the urea is removed. The investigator is testing whether the information needed to specify the enzyme''s folded shape resides in the polypeptide itself. Provided refolding is allowed to reach its most stable state, this protocol is most likely to show that the enzyme', '[{"label": "A", "text": "loses its primary structure during unfolding"}, {"label": "B", "text": "requires a separate template to refold"}, {"label": "C", "text": "regains activity only as multiple subunits reassemble"}, {"label": "D", "text": "reforms its native disulfides and recovers activity"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is D because in Anfinsen''s classic ribonuclease experiment, urea and \(\beta\)-mercaptoethanol unfold and reduce the enzyme, and once the reducing agent is removed under oxidizing conditions the disulfides reform and the denaturant is removed, the chain spontaneously refolds; allowed to reach its most stable set of bonds, it regains full activity. The textbook states that this showed "all the necessary and sufficient information required to direct the folding of a protein into the native state is present in the primary sequence" and that "He then removed the βME using dialysis, allowing the disulfides to reform. Next, he removed the denaturing reagent, urea... if, however, he added catalytic amounts of βME, the protein soon retained 100% of its initial activity" (libretexts-biochemistry.txt lines 13813-13832). Because the native disulfides are tertiary-structure bonds that re-pair correctly as the chain refolds, function returns without any external instructions. (Choice A) is incorrect: denaturation disrupts the 3D (tertiary/quaternary) fold but does not break the peptide bonds, so "the order and nature of amino acids in the primary sequence... determine the folding pattern" is retained throughout (lines 6435, 12678-12685). (Choice B) is incorrect: the entire point is that no external template is needed — "the fixed 3D structure... is uniquely encoded in their primary structure (the amino acid sequence)" (lines 12678-12685). (Choice C) is incorrect: ribonuclease is a single-chain protein, so quaternary subunit reassembly is irrelevant; quaternary structure applies only when "separate protein chains... interact" to form dimers and larger assemblies (lines 6597-6599, 13784). (Choice D) is correct: removing the reducing agent lets the cysteine thiols re-oxidize into the native disulfides (RSSR is the oxidized form of thiols), and refolding restores activity, demonstrating that the primary sequence encodes the native fold (lines 5892-5894, 13813-13832). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between primary sequence, disulfide reformation, and recovered function to predict the outcome of a defined unfolding-and-refolding protocol.', 'medium', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 110)
  RETURNING id
)
