-- Q25 [Conjugated Proteins & Denaturation] hard skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Conjugated Proteins & Denaturation', 'A biochemist treats a purified globular protein with several denaturing agents and tracks which stabilizing interactions are lost. Considering the chemistry of each treatment, which of the following statements about how a denaturing agent acts is accurate?

I. A reducing agent such as beta-mercaptoethanol unfolds the protein by hydrolyzing its backbone peptide bonds.
II. Heating the protein to near-boiling cleaves its covalent disulfide bonds while leaving hydrogen bonds intact.
III. Urea or guanidinium chloride unfolds the protein by disrupting the noncovalent interactions that bury hydrophobic residues.', '[{"label": "A", "text": "I only"}, {"label": "B", "text": "III only"}, {"label": "C", "text": "I and III only"}, {"label": "D", "text": "II and III only"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is B because only statement III correctly pairs a denaturing agent with the interaction it disrupts. Denaturation is the loss of three-dimensional (tertiary and quaternary) structure and function without breaking the primary sequence; each agent disrupts a specific class of stabilizing interaction (OpenStax 4475-4485). Urea and guanidinium chloride unfold proteins through generalized solvent effects: "Other salts, such as guanidinium chloride, unfold proteins without causing precipitation" and urea acts "through generalized solvent effects or nonspecific interactions with the protein," weakening the noncovalent hydrophobic effect that buries nonpolar side chains in the protein core (LibreTexts 6837, 13827, 4988). (Choice A) is incorrect: beta-mercaptoethanol is a disulfide-reducing agent that cleaves the covalent \(-\text{S}-\text{S}-\) bond of cystine back to two free thiols; it does not hydrolyze backbone peptide bonds, and denaturation leaves the primary sequence intact (LibreTexts 5953-5954, 13825). (Choice B) is correct: guanidinium chloride and urea unfold proteins by disrupting the noncovalent interactions, including the hydrophobic effect that sequesters nonpolar residues away from water (LibreTexts 6837, 13827, 4988). (Choice C) is incorrect: it pairs the true statement III with the false statement I, which wrongly assigns peptide-bond hydrolysis to a reducing agent. (Choice D) is incorrect: statement II reverses the chemistry—heat disrupts the weak noncovalent interactions (hydrogen bonds and hydrophobic packing), whereas the covalent disulfide bond is far more resistant and is the target of reducing agents, not heat (LibreTexts 5953-5954, 11247). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the distinction between covalent and noncovalent stabilizing interactions to predict which bonds each denaturing treatment breaks.', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
